[section:gauss_laguerre Gauss-Laguerre quadrature]

[heading Synopsis]

`#include <boost/math/quadrature/gauss_laguerre.hpp>`

   namespace boost{ namespace math{ namespace quadrature{

   template <class Real, unsigned Points, class ``__Policy`` = boost::math::policies::policy<> >
   class gauss_laguerre
   {
   public:
      explicit gauss_laguerre(Real alpha = 0);

      const std::vector<Real>& abscissa() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& weights() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_abscissa() const;
      const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_weights() const;

      template <class F>
      auto integrate(F f, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

      template <class F, class Norm>
      auto integrate(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

      template <class F>
      auto integrate_averaged(F f, Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

      template <class F, class Norm>
      auto integrate_averaged(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm,
                              Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));
   };

   }}} // namespaces

[heading Description]

The `gauss_laguerre` class template computes

[expression ['[int][sub 0][super [infin]] f(x) x[super [alpha]] e[super -x] dx [approx] [sum][sub i] w[sub i] f(x[sub i])]]

using /Points/ evaluations of /f/, where the abscissas /x/[sub i] are the zeros of the generalized Laguerre polynomial
/L/[sub /Points/][super ([alpha])], which [link math_toolkit.sf_poly.laguerre `laguerre_zeros`] also returns.
The rule is exact when /f/ is a polynomial of degree at most 2['Points] - 1.
Note that the weight function /x/[super [alpha]] exp(-/x/) is not part of /f/: pass only the factor that multiplies it.
This is what makes the rule effective for integrands with an integrable singularity /x/[super [alpha]] at the origin.

Like [link math_toolkit.gauss `gauss`], this is a "one shot" non-adaptive routine,
intended for smooth /f/ that grow no faster than a polynomial,
such as moments and Laplace transforms of smooth functions, or expectations under a gamma distribution.
For /Points/ + 1 more evaluations of /f/, `integrate_averaged` is exact to higher degree and estimates its error.
/Points/ must be at least 2.

   explicit gauss_laguerre(Real alpha = 0);

Constructs the rule for the weight function /x/[super [alpha]] exp(-/x/), which requires [alpha] to be finite and greater than -1:
otherwise the constructor raises a __domain_error through the /Policy/.
If the policy does not throw, the abscissas and weights are NaN, and so is every integral.
For [alpha] = 0 the rule is shared by all objects of the same type: it comes from the pre-computed tables or is computed once and cached.
For other values of [alpha] the constructor computes the rule.

   const std::vector<Real>& abscissa() const;
   const std::vector<Real>& weights() const;

These provide direct access to the abscissas, all positive and in increasing order, and their weights.

   const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_abscissa() const;
   const std::vector<Real>& anti_gauss_weights() const;

These provide direct access to the nodes and weights of the anti-Gauss rule /A/ of `integrate_averaged`, described below.
It is computed the first time it is needed, then cached.

   template <class F>
   auto integrate(F f, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

Integrates /f/(/x/) /x/[super [alpha]] exp(-/x/) over \[0, [infin]) with the /Points/-point Gauss rule /G/. /f/ may return a real or complex value.

If `pL1` is not null, `*pL1` is set to the corresponding integral of |/f/|.
If that is substantially larger than the absolute value of the result, then the sum was ill-conditioned.

   template <class F, class Norm>
   auto integrate(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

As for [link math_toolkit.gauss `gauss`], this overload handles vector- and matrix-valued integrands:
`zero` is the additive identity of the returned type, and `norm` returns a nonnegative scalar convertible to `Real`.
The L1 norm is measured with `norm`.

   template <class F>
   auto integrate_averaged(F f, Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(std::declval<F>()(std::declval<Real>()));

   template <class F, class Norm>
   auto integrate_averaged(F f, const decltype(f(Real(0)))& zero, Norm norm,
                           Real* error = nullptr, Real* pL1 = nullptr) const -> decltype(f(Real(0)));

Returns Laurie's averaged rule (/G/(/f/) + /A/(/f/))/2, at the cost of /Points/ + 1 more evaluations of /f/.
/A/ is the anti-Gauss rule: the rule with /Points/ + 1 nodes whose error on every polynomial of degree up to 2['Points] + 1
is exactly that of /G/ with the opposite sign, so the average is exact for polynomials of degree 2['Points] + 1.
Its nodes interlace the Gauss nodes, all of them are positive, and its weights are positive.

If `error` is not null, `*error` is set to |/A/(/f/) - /G/(/f/)|/2, but never less than 2[epsilon] times the L1 norm.
When /G/ and /A/ bracket the integral, as they do for smooth /f/ once the error falls steadily with /Points/, this bounds the error of the average;
it is then also close to the error of /G/.
Otherwise it is a heuristic, and integrands that alias onto the null space of /A/ - /G/ can fool it.
If `pL1` is not null, `*pL1` is set to the L1 norm of the averaged rule, measured with `norm` in the second overload.

[heading Example]

The integral

[expression ['[int][sub 0][super [infin]] x[super -1/2] e[super -x] cos(x) dx = [Gamma](1/2) cos([pi]/8) / 2[super 1/4]]]

has a singularity at the origin, which the weight function absorbs with [alpha] = -1/2:

   using boost::math::quadrature::gauss_laguerre;
   gauss_laguerre<double, 30> rule(-0.5);
   double error;
   double I = rule.integrate_averaged([](double x) { return std::cos(x); }, &error);

`I` is the exact value rounded to double precision, and `error` is about 7e-16.
With 15 points the error of `I` is 9e-13, and `error`, 9e-12, bounds it.

[heading Choosing the number of points]

`gauss_laguerre` has pre-computed tables of abscissas and weights for [alpha] = 0 and 7, 10 and 15 points, at up to 110 decimal digits of precision.
For other numbers of points, or higher precision types, or [alpha] [ne] 0, the abscissas and weights are computed:
they are found in O(/Points/) operations by following the differential equation of the Laguerre functions
from zero to zero in twice the working precision (Glaser, Liu and Rokhlin); the weights of the largest abscissas underflow for large /Points/.
Where that method cannot vouch for a zero, the rule comes from the three-term recurrence instead, which overflows for large /Points/ combined with
large [alpha] (in `double`, for example 2000 points and [alpha] = 50).
If the rule cannot be computed, `integrate` and `integrate_averaged` raise an __evaluation_error through the /Policy/;
if the policy does not throw, the result is a NaN.

As with `gauss`, defining `BOOST_MATH_GAUSS_NO_COMPUTE_ON_DEMAND` turns any combination of type and number of points without
pre-computed values into a compile-time error.

[heading References]

* G. H. Golub and J. H. Welsch, ['Calculation of Gauss quadrature rules], Mathematics of Computation 23 (1969), 221-230.
* A. Glaser, X. Liu and V. Rokhlin, ['A fast algorithm for the calculation of the roots of special functions], SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 29 (2007), no. 4, 1420-1438, [@https://doi.org/10.1137/06067016X doi:10.1137/06067016X].
* W. Gautschi, ['Orthogonal Polynomials: Computation and Approximation], Oxford University Press, 2004.
* D. P. Laurie, ['Anti-Gaussian quadrature formulas], Mathematics of Computation 65 (1996), no. 214, 739-747, [@https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-96-00713-2 doi:10.1090/S0025-5718-96-00713-2].

[endsect] [/section:gauss_laguerre Gauss-Laguerre quadrature]
